    Registered(
      name: "ui",
      package: pkgUi.package,
      summary:
        "How the program looks: units, intervals, styles and themes, tables and the live display.",
      doc:
        """How the program looks: units, intervals, styles and themes, tables and the live display.

* The number formats every listing needs (`units`): the unit follows the number with no space (`36.7km`, `+89m`, `17.8%`,
  `1h41m`), and a value that is not recorded is a dash.
* Intervals of time (`interval`): units `d h m s`, no leading zero unit, the first unpadded and later ones two digits
  (`45s`, `22m05s`, `4h41m`, `2d03h07m`); seconds only under an hour; rounding carries.
* Styles (`style`, `vocabulary`, `markup`): text is in named styles, and what each looks like is the theme's
  (`core/theme.nim`: foreground, background, weight, underline, italic, reverse, each optional; the built-in `dark.txt`
  and `light.txt` are here, and the names the program uses are those of `dark.txt`, checked when it is compiled).  The
  words and styles for what happened are in `vocabulary`, one table, so that "failed" is `bad` wherever it appears.
  Style only where it means something: `good` (done), `bad` (failed or deleted), `warn` (advisory), `select` (the
  selection, `selectedStyle`), each used for nothing else; `em` for what to read first, `hide` for the bookkeeping and
  what was not recorded; `default` is around everything.  Markup (`[good imported] 3 files`) gives text its styles,
  which nest; `tools/look.nim` shows text with a theme.  (The web pages' colours are still `palette`'s: TODO "Styles,
  themes and markup", step 5.)
* A table's cells lined up in columns (`columns`), and a block of lines redrawn in place on a terminal (`live`), which also
  says how wide the help text may be (`helpWidth`).""",
    ),
    Registered(
      name: "user",
      package: pkgUser.package,
      summary: "The users, and which one a command works for.",
      doc: """The users, and which one a command works for.

Very lightweight: people sharing a computer.  This package owns the tables `user` and `user_profile` (the user's threshold
heart rate, `fthr`), and every other package's per-user table refers to `user` and so depends on this package.  Exactly one
user is the default, the one a command works on without `--user`; the first is made when a database is first opened, and
if the default is deleted another becomes it.  Deleting a user deletes everything they have, by `on delete cascade`
(`wipe.userTables` lists the tables, and a test checks that none is forgotten).

* `openSession` finds the user a command works for and gives a `Session` (the store, the user's id and name); a `Session`
  converts to a `Store`, so only code that needs the user says `Session`.
* `nch users` is a command of this package: make, rename, set the FTHR of users, choose the default, delete.  A user's name
  is the key for `--user`, and users are never made by accident (`--add`).  Changing an FTHR makes `model` work its
  impulses out again, by a trigger of its own.
* The extension point `user/has`: the packages whose data hangs off a user contribute a count with its name (`importer`:
  the activities).  `nch users` shows each as a column and says them when it asks before a delete.""",
    ),
    Registered(
      name: "args",
      package: pkgArgs.package,
      summary:
        "One command-line parser for all the commands, and the usage text made from the same descriptions.",
      doc:
        """One command-line parser for all the commands, and the usage text made from the same descriptions.

A command describes its options as a list (`Opt`s made with `flag`, `text`, `number`, `whole`, `custom` and
`listOption`).  Each option carries its description, which the compiler requires: its names, the placeholder for its
value, its help, whether it is indented under the option before it (`level`: `--rename` under `--edit`), and what could
complete it (`OptDoc`).  The option list of a command's usage is made from the descriptions (`usage.nim`), wrapped to the
width of the terminal (`ui/live.helpWidth`), so it cannot differ from what the parser accepts; only the synopsis, the
introduction and the notes are written by hand.  A package that offers an option to another command (the criteria of
`activities`) hands over just the description.

`parseCommand` reads the options into a builder and the command's own `finish` turns it into the answer: a `Parsed[T]`,
which is valid parameters (a variant on the action, so that a combination of options that means nothing cannot be
represented), a request for help, or a problem (a sentence that does not name the option).  An option's `apply` returns
`fine` or `refuse("...")`.  An option may also say it is allowed in an address of the local web server (`address`, with
a check for its value); the server's list of what a page may be given is made from the options themselves.""",
